ts across the window:** Every member of the review group interacts with essentially the same fixed \"core\" partner set \u2014 {18, 61, 189, 212, 232, 309, 339, 381, 566, 633, 647, 684, 698, 702, 706, 719, 739, 768, 976, 1082, 1084, 1303, 1360, 1466, 1485, 1595, 1710, 1810, 1872, 1964} \u2014 and this set is present *both* before and after every candidate change point:\n\n- Before 54607: node 431 (step_14), 596 (step_16), 807 (step_21), 900 (step_22) all contact exactly that core set.\n- After 54607: node 923 (step_31), 930 (step_11), 1064 (step_12) contact exactly the same core set.\n\nSo the individual members do show sharp on/off bursts (e.g. 930 has only 4 partners in 39607\u201349607 and 31 in 49607\u201359607, step_04/step_05; 431 goes to zero after 54607, step_15; 1064 goes to zero after 64607, step_13), but the *identity* of the contacts does not change \u2014 the \"new\" contacts of a late-activating member are exactly the \"old\" contacts of an early-activating member. There is no old-set \u2192 new-set substitution, only a staggered relay onto a persistent hub set.\n\n**Confounder controls:** The core partners' own activity is flat across all six sub-windows (step_29: 66/63/42/54/62/58; step_30: 66/63/48/46/68/59), so there is no cohort of contacts that appears or disappears. Group volume is likewise roughly flat (step_03), with dips only at the window seams, and core partners are moderately (not newly) popular (step_28).\n\n```json\n{\n  \"task_id\": \"t_d9748f02\",\n  \"decision\": \"absent\",\n  \"mechanism\": \"regime_switch\",\n  \"entities\": [431, 596, 653, 759, 807, 900, 923, 930, 1064, 1159, 1663, 1783],\n  \"window\": [39607, 69607],\n  \"confidence\": 0.82,\n  \"evidence\": [\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_14\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_16\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_21\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_22\", \"role\": \"support\"},\n    {\"trace_id\": \"step_11\", \"role\": \"